# pylonforce2mqtt

Exposes a Pylontech Force H2 battery to MQTT following the [Homie 5
convention](https://homieiot.github.io/). Talks to the battery's BMS over Modbus
RTU over TCP (such as what's exposed by the logger built into the BMS).

See [REGISTERS.md](REGISTERS.md) for the register map.

## Usage

```
pylonforce2mqtt mqtt --tcp gateway.local:502 --mqtt-broker mqtt://broker:1883?client_id=pylonforce
```

Nothing is ever written to the battery, and nothing is subscribed to on MQTT: all of the properties
are read-only.

`--tcp` is the address of the gateway, expected to relay raw Modbus RTU frames. `--unit-id` is the Modbus address of the battery (1 by default.)
`PYLONFORCE2MQTT_LOG=debug` makes it more talkative.

Options:

* `--poll-period` (5 seconds by default) is how often the state of the battery is read. Who the
  battery is (its name, version and serial number) is read every 10 minutes instead.
* `--detailed` also publishes the voltage of every cell, see below.
* `--device-id` (`pylonforce` by default) is the ID of the bridge device, see [Devices](#devices).
* `--unavailable-after` (60 seconds by default) is for how long the battery can go without a single
  successful read before the stack is `disconnected`.

## Devices

* **`pylonforce`** is the bridge: the root device, without nodes.
* **`pylonforce-<serial number>`** is the battery, as one device and the child of the bridge. The
  serial number is lowercased, with each run of anything but letters and digits made into a hyphen
  (`AB1234-56/7` gives `pylonforce-ab1234-56-7`). It is named after the manufacturer and
  the model, e.g. `Pylon Force_H2` (`Pylontech Force H2` if the battery does not tell.)

Stack is only published after the serial number has been read; the bridge can appear `ready` without
children until then. If the serial number changes (the battery has been replaced) the bridge has the
new stack as its only child.

### Nodes and properties of the stack

| Node | Property | Type | Unit | Decimals | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `stack` | `state` | enum | | | `idle`, `charging`, `discharging` or `sleep` |
| | `voltage` | float | V | 1 | |
| | `current` | float | A | 2 | positive when charging |
| | `power` | float | W | 0 | voltage times current |
| | `state-of-charge` | integer | % | | `0:100` |
| | `state-of-health` | integer | % | | `0:100` |
| | `remaining-energy` | float | kWh | 3 | |
| | `cycles` | integer | # | | |
| | `max-temperature`, `min-temperature` | float | °C | 1 | of the modules |
| | `max-temperature-module`, `min-temperature-module` | integer | | | the module, counted from 1 |
| `cells` | `max-voltage`, `min-voltage` | float | V | 3 | |
| | `voltage-spread` | float | V | 3 | highest minus lowest |
| | `average-voltage` | float | V | 4 | made out of the voltages of the modules |
| | `max-voltage-module`, `max-voltage-cell`, `min-voltage-module`, `min-voltage-cell` | integer | | | the module and the cell in it, counted from 1 |
| `limits` | `charge-voltage`, `discharge-voltage` | float | V | 1 | what the BMS requests of the inverter |
| | `charge-current`, `discharge-current` | float | A | 2 | likewise (positive, for both) |
| | `charge-allowed`, `discharge-allowed` | boolean | | | |
| | `force-charge-request`, `balance-charge-request` | boolean | | | |
| `energy` | `charged-today`, `discharged-today` | float | kWh | 3 | |
| | `charged-total`, `discharged-total` | float | kWh | 0 | see the caveats |
| `health` | `protections`, `alarms`, `faults` | JSON | | | a sorted array of the names of the conditions, `[]` if there are none |
| `bms` | `temperature` | float | °C | 1 | of the BMS itself, not of the cells |
| | `firmware-version` | string | | | e.g. `2.0 (B52.34)` |
| | `serial-number` | string | | | |
| `module-N` | `voltage` | float | V | 2 | for each of the modules, counted from 1 |
| | `average-cell-voltage` | float | V | 4 | the voltage of the module over the number of cells in it |
| | `temperature` | float | °C | 1 | |
| | `max-cell-voltage`, `min-cell-voltage` | float | V | 3 | only with `--detailed`; of the cells of the module |
| | `max-cell-voltage-cell`, `min-cell-voltage-cell` | integer | | | only with `--detailed`; the cell in the module, counted from 1 (the first one if there are several) |
| | `cell-voltage-spread` | float | V | 3 | only with `--detailed`; highest minus lowest of the module |
| `module-N-cell-K` | `voltage` | float | V | 3 | only with `--detailed` |

A value that is not valid (a state of charge above 100 %, a cell at 0 V, a flag that is neither 0
nor 1 and so on) is not published: the last valid value stays. This is logged as a warning when it
starts, and at info when the value is valid again.

The number of modules and cells are read from the battery and are valid with 1 to 6 modules, 1 to
150 cells and the same number of cells in every module. The last valid numbers are what the nodes
are made of. If there never are any, the stack has no `module-N` nodes and no properties that need
to know which cell is in which module (`average-voltage`, the `*-module` and `*-cell` ones.)

### `--detailed`

Adds the voltage of every cell as a property of a node of its own, `module-N-cell-K`, and the
`--detailed` properties of the modules, which are made out of the voltages of the cells (not valid
if any cell of the module is at 0 V). The battery can have 150 cells, which makes for a large
device description, so this is off by default, and the registers with the voltages of the cells are
not even polled without it. The temperature of the cells is never published as it is just the
module temperature.

## Caveats

* Only one battery, with 4 modules of 30 cells, has been tested. Other firmware versions and
  batteries may behave differently.
* The total energy counters (`charged-total` and `discharged-total`) occasionally jump by huge
  amounts. This is possibly a firmware bug. Do not trust them.
* The temperatures have the resolution of 1 °C, even if they are published with a decimal.
* `bms/temperature` is the temperature of the BMS itself and not of the cells. It reads high,
  compared to them.
* At very low currents the firmware flips the `state` back and forth between `idle` and
  `discharging`.
* Reads are one at a time with a pause between them, as the bus is slow. The status is read in a
  single request so that properties derived from multiple registers are consistent.
